{
  "available": true,
  "question": "Do moon phase, Chinese lunar date, perigee, or a geometric eclipse line up with Powerball or Mega Millions draws?",
  "answer_in_brief": "No. Moon phase does not line up with these draws: none of the association tests fell under p = 0.05. Two lunar-model scores beat a random ticket before correction, and Benjamini\u2013Hochberg keeps none of them.",
  "summary": "10 tests asked whether moon phase lines up with the numbers. If every null were true, about 0.50 of them would fall under p = 0.05 by luck. 0 did. Benjamini\u2013Hochberg kept 0; Bonferroni kept 0. Permutation p-values cannot go below 0.0005 with this shuffle count. Powerball: 1426 draws from 2015-10-07 to 2026-10-28. The smallest permutation p-value is 0.210 for White-ball numbers by moon phase (cramers_v 0.0972). Benjamini\u2013Hochberg keeps 0 of 5 tests in this game. On 282 held-out draws a random ticket expects 0.362 white-ball matches and 10.85 Powerball hits. The plain model averaged 0.394 white matches and hit the Powerball 14 times. The lunar model averaged 0.298 (versus chance, p 0.055) and hit the Powerball 19 times (p 0.019). One seeded random ticket on the same draws averaged 0.387 white matches and hit the Powerball 11 times. Adding the moon gave the lunar model fewer white matches than the plain model (-0.096 per draw, p 0.013, Cohen's d -0.15). Bonus MAE is 6.84 with the moon and 6.79 without it, against 8.89 for a uniform guess. A guess near the middle of the range beats a uniform draw on absolute error even when it does not hit the number. Mega Millions: 939 draws from 2017-10-31 to 2026-10-27. The smallest permutation p-value is 0.086 for High white balls by moon phase (eta_squared 0.0134, phase means differ by 0.427). Benjamini\u2013Hochberg keeps 0 of 5 tests in this game. On 184 held-out draws a random ticket expects 0.357 white-ball matches and 7.36 Mega Ball hits. The plain model averaged 0.310 white matches and hit the Mega Ball 6 times. The lunar model averaged 0.451 (versus chance, p 0.044) and hit the Mega Ball 8 times (p 0.707). One seeded random ticket on the same draws averaged 0.353 white matches and hit the Mega Ball 16 times. Adding the moon gave the lunar model more white matches than the plain model (+0.141 per draw, p 0.007, Cohen's d +0.20). Bonus MAE is 6.33 with the moon and 6.36 without it, against 8.36 for a uniform guess. A guess near the middle of the range beats a uniform draw on absolute error even when it does not hit the number. The four lunar-model scores against a random ticket (white matches and bonus hits, two games) had 2 raw p-values under 0.05. Benjamini\u2013Hochberg kept 0 of them. The paired Powerball and Mega Millions differences point in opposite directions, so they are not one lunar effect. Nothing in this sample survives a multiple-testing correction. That is what random draws look like. The moon is not a reason to expect a number.",
  "caveat": "These are association tests and a small neural net, not a betting system. Draws are designed to be random. Features are the moon at 22:59 America/New_York on the draw date, which is known before the drawing.",
